## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical design of a privacy-conscious, end-to-end streaming pipeline that validates, masks, deduplicates, and persists data with observability.

## Key Concepts
- Kinesis Data Streams, Lambda, DynamoDB dedup, S3 Parquet storage, DLQ
- JSON schema validation, field-level masking of PII, correlationId
- Backfill strategy, LocalStack testing, basic observability (CloudWatch, X-Ray)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maskPII(record) {
  const r = {...record};
  if (r.email) r.email = r.email.replace(/[^@]+@/, '***@');
  if (r.phone) r.phone = r.phone.length > 4 ? r.phone.slice(0, 3) + '****' + r.phone.slice(-4) : r.phone;
  if (r.ssn) r.ssn = '***-**-' + r.ssn.slice(-4);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the masking logic end-to-end, including replays?
- How would you ensure no PII leaks to DLQ or downstream sinks?
- How would you scale this to higher event rates or additional device classes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of practical data validation, versioned schemas, and end-to-end observability in a streaming pipeline. It also exercises handling of invalid data with a DLQ and a backfill workflow, plus LocalStack testing.

## Key Concepts
- Kinesis Data Streams
- DynamoDB as a lightweight schema registry
- Lambda data validation
- S3 Parquet storage with partitions
- DLQ handling
- X-Ray tracing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js pseudo-code for validator
const registry = getRegistryFromDynamoDB();
function validateEvent(evt){
  const v = evt.version;
  const rules = registry[v];
  if(!rules) return {ok:false, reason:'unsupported version'};
  // basic field checks per rules
  return {ok:true};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this setup using LocalStack or mocks?
- How would you evolve the schema while preserving backwards compatibility?
